            {"heading": "Somatic Memory", "body": ["Research in developmental trauma and somatic psychology has established clearly: early experiences are encoded in the nervous system, in habitual muscle tension patterns, in breath patterns, in how the body holds itself. Talk therapy alone often doesn't reach this level of encoding.", "Body-oriented practices — somatic experiencing, EMDR, yoga, breathwork, expressive movement — access the body-stored aspects of early experience. They are not replacements for psychological work; they are complements that make psychological work more complete.", "Even without formal somatic therapy, noticing where in your body you feel childhood-wound emotions (the chest tightness of abandonment, the throat constriction of shame, the shoulder bracing of hypervigilance) and consciously breathing into those spaces is a beginning."]},
