b2 = {
    "title": "The Boundaries Blueprint",
    "subtitle": "How to Say No Without Guilt and Yes Without Resentment",
    "series_name": SERIES, "accent_hex": ACCENT, "bg_hex": BG,
    "disclaimer": DISCLAIMER,
    "chapters": [
        {"title": "What Boundaries Actually Are", "intro": "Boundaries are not walls. They are not punishments. They are not rejection. They are a description of what works for you.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "The Misunderstanding", "body": ["Most people think of boundaries as aggressive or cold — something you do to people who've wronged you, or a way of keeping people at arm's length. This framing makes them feel unnatural, unkind, or reserved for severe situations.", "Boundaries are simply communication about your needs, limits, and values. 'I can't take on any more projects this month' is a boundary. 'I need 30 minutes alone after work before I'm available for conversation' is a boundary. 'I'm not comfortable with that kind of humor' is a boundary.", "None of these are hostile. They are honest. And people who care about you would rather know than not know."]},
        ], "callout": "Boundaries are information, not rejection."},
        {"title": "Why Boundaries Feel So Hard", "intro": "If boundaries were easy, everyone would have them. The difficulty is almost always rooted in something older than the current situation.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "The Roots of Boundary Difficulty", "body": ["Boundaries are hardest for people who grew up in environments where: their needs were treated as burdens, saying no produced consequences, their emotional job was to manage others' feelings, love felt conditional on compliance, or conflict was genuinely unsafe.", "These experiences produce adults who feel deep guilt when saying no, who read normal boundary-setting as abandonment or cruelty, who compulsively override their own needs to prevent others' discomfort — and who eventually feel profoundly resentful and depleted.", "Understanding this root doesn't eliminate the difficulty. But it explains why 'just say no' advice feels impossible — it's not a skill gap, it's a fear response."]},
        ], "callout": "Difficulty with boundaries is almost always a trauma response, not a character flaw."},
        {"title": "The Guilt — Working Through It", "intro": "Boundary guilt is almost never a sign that you've done something wrong. It's a sign that you've done something unfamiliar.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "Separating Guilt from Conscience", "body": ["Genuine conscience says: you've caused real harm to someone who didn't deserve it. Adapt your behavior.", "Boundary guilt says: someone is disappointed or displeased, which means you've failed. This is not conscience — it is the internalized voice of an environment that trained you to take responsibility for others' emotional states.", "When you feel guilty after setting a healthy boundary, practice asking: have I actually caused harm? Or am I just experiencing the discomfort of someone's disappointment?", "If the answer is the latter, the work is to tolerate the feeling without reversing the boundary. Guilt that is allowed to exist without being acted on eventually diminishes. Guilt that is always appeased through compliance grows stronger."]},
        ], "callout": "Tolerate the guilt. Don't let it run your decisions."},
        {"title": "How to Communicate Limits Clearly", "intro": "Vague limits invite interpretation. Clear communication is kind, not harsh.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "The Simple Script", "body": ["You don't need a long explanation. 'I'm not available for calls after 7pm' needs no justification. 'I can't take on that project right now' requires no excuse.", "The more you explain and justify, the more you invite negotiation. A boundary stated simply and calmly signals confidence. A boundary buried in apology and qualification signals ambivalence that some people will try to exploit.", "If someone consistently pushes against your boundaries despite clear communication, that is information about them — about whether this relationship has the qualities that allow your authentic self to exist in it."]},
        ], "callout": "Over-explaining a boundary weakens it."},
        {"title": "Boundaries in Specific Relationships", "intro": "Different relationships have different boundary architectures.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "Family, Work, and Romantic Relationships", "body": ["Family: family-of-origin relationships often have the deepest boundary violations and the most emotional complexity. Boundaries with family sometimes require starting very small and being prepared for significant resistance before things stabilize.", "Work: professional boundaries include availability outside work hours, emotional labor limits, and capacity management. The culture increasingly normalizes 24/7 availability; resisting this requires clarity about your own professional standards.", "Romantic relationships: boundaries in intimate relationships are not about distance — they are about preserving two distinct individuals in genuine connection. A relationship where both people have dissolved their individuality to accommodate the other is enmeshed, not close."]},
        ], "callout": "Good relationships have room for two whole people."},
        {"title": "When Others Don't Respect Your Limits", "intro": "The response to boundary violations tells you more about a relationship than the violation itself.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "Evaluating the Response", "body": ["When you communicate a clear, reasonable limit, healthy people respond with: acknowledgment, even if they're disappointed. Brief disappointment that resolves. Curiosity about your needs. Adaptation.", "Unhealthy responses include: manipulation, guilt-tripping, punishing you with silence, escalating demands, dismissing your need as unreasonable, or consistent 'forgetting.'", "Repeated, sustained disrespect of clearly communicated limits is information about the relationship. It is not an invitation to set the limit differently, justify it more, or work harder to earn being treated well. It is information about whether this relationship has space for your whole self."]},
        ], "callout": "The response to your boundary is data about the relationship."},
    ]
}

# ── Book 3 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
b3 = {
    "title": "Self-Compassion in Practice",
    "subtitle": "How to Be on Your Own Side",
    "series_name": SERIES, "accent_hex": ACCENT, "bg_hex": BG,
    "disclaimer": DISCLAIMER,
    "chapters": [
        {"title": "Why Being Kind to Yourself Is Harder Than Being Kind to Others", "intro": "Most people apply a double standard: grace for others, judgment for themselves. Understanding why reveals the path forward.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "The Inner Critic's Job", "body": ["The inner critic — that relentless voice that catalogs your failures, anticipates your inadequacy, and compares you unfavorably to everyone around you — is not random cruelty. It has a job: to motivate you to be better, to protect you from the pain of failure, to keep you small enough to stay safe.", "The problem is that the inner critic is spectacularly bad at its job. Research by Kristin Neff and others shows consistently that self-criticism increases anxiety, decreases motivation, reduces resilience after failure, and produces worse outcomes than self-compassion on virtually every metric it's trying to improve.", "The inner critic is trying to help. It's using the wrong method. Self-compassion is not the absence of standards — it's a more effective way to meet them."]},
        ], "callout": "Self-criticism doesn't make you better. Self-compassion does."},
        {"title": "The Three Components of Self-Compassion", "intro": "Kristin Neff's framework offers a practical structure for understanding and practicing self-compassion.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "Mindfulness, Common Humanity, Self-Kindness", "body": ["Mindfulness: seeing clearly what is happening — the pain, the struggle, the mistake — without exaggerating it or suppressing it. Just: this is hard. This hurts. I made a mistake.", "Common humanity: recognizing that struggle, imperfection, and suffering are universal human experiences — not personal failures that set you apart. You are not the only person who has felt this. You are not uniquely broken.", "Self-kindness: responding to your own pain with the warmth you would offer a good friend. Not indulgence, not bypassing accountability — just warmth. 'This is hard. You're doing your best. It's okay to struggle with this.'", "These three components work together. Mindfulness without warmth is cold observation. Warmth without honesty is false comfort. Common humanity prevents the isolation that shame thrives in."]},
        ], "callout": "What would you say to a good friend in this situation? Say it to yourself."},
        {"title": "Self-Compassion Is Not Self-Pity", "intro": "The most common objection to self-compassion: 'won't I just wallow and give up?' The research says no.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "What the Evidence Shows", "body": ["People who score high on self-compassion take more personal responsibility for their mistakes (not less), because they can look at mistakes honestly without being overwhelmed by shame that requires denial.", "They are more motivated to try again after failure — the self-compassionate response to failure is 'I can learn from this and try again' rather than 'I'm fundamentally inadequate and should stop.'", "They have lower rates of anxiety and depression, higher emotional resilience, and better relationship quality. The worried belief that self-compassion produces laziness or low standards is directly contradicted by research."]},
        ], "callout": "Self-compassion produces more growth than self-criticism. Every time."},
        {"title": "Practices for Building the Habit", "intro": "Self-compassion is a practice, not a state you achieve once.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "Daily Practices", "body": ["The self-compassion pause: when you notice self-critical thoughts, pause and ask: how would I talk to a friend going through exactly this? Then say that to yourself. Out loud if possible.", "Hand on heart: placing a hand on your chest and breathing slowly while acknowledging your difficulty activates the same physiological calming as receiving physical comfort. It sounds simple. It works.", "The three-step phrase: in moments of struggle, say to yourself: 'This is a moment of suffering. Suffering is part of life. May I be kind to myself in this moment.' The repetition and structure interrupt the shame spiral before it escalates."]},
        ], "callout": "The pause before the self-judgment is where the practice lives."},
        {"title": "Self-Compassion and Performance", "intro": "The counterintuitive truth: self-compassion in high-performance contexts produces better results than self-criticism.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "Athletes, Professionals, and Perfectionists", "body": ["Perfectionism and high standards are often defended as necessary for excellence. Research consistently shows the opposite: perfectionistic self-criticism is associated with burnout, reduced creativity, decreased resilience to setbacks, and ultimately lower performance than a high-standards-with-self-compassion approach.", "The athlete who can recover from a bad performance with self-compassion — 'that wasn't my best, I can analyze it and do better' — outperforms the one spiraling in shame. The professional who can acknowledge mistakes clearly and course-correct without a shame crisis learns faster.", "Self-compassion is not a soft skill. In high-performance contexts, it may be the most practically important one."]},
        ], "callout": "High standards and self-compassion are not in conflict. They're partners."},
        {"title": "When Self-Compassion Meets Deep Pain", "intro": "For some people, self-compassion touches something very old and very painful. This is not failure — it's progress.", "sections": [
            {"heading": "The Backdraft", "body": ["Some people, when they first practice self-kindness, find that it triggers grief, sadness, or intense emotion rather than relief. This is called 'backdraft' in self-compassion research — when you finally open the door to self-warmth, the pain that has been waiting there floods through.", "This is a sign that the practice is reaching something real. The appropriate response is not to stop — it is to slow down, moderate the intensity, and if needed, work with a therapist.", "If you find that self-compassion consistently produces overwhelming emotion, professional support in developing the skill is appropriate. This is not a sign that you need more willpower — it's a sign that the wounds are significant enough to deserve skilled companionship."]},
        ], "callout": "If self-compassion breaks you open, you've found what needed tending."},
    ]
}

