            {"heading": "Why Granularity Matters", "body": ["Research by Lisa Feldman Barrett shows that people who can distinguish between emotional states more precisely — who don't just feel 'bad' but can identify whether they're anxious, disappointed, embarrassed, resentful, or overwhelmed — have better emotional regulation outcomes, make better decisions under stress, and have better physical health outcomes.", "Emotion granularity is built by expanding your emotional vocabulary and practicing precise naming. Not 'I'm stressed' but 'I'm dreading this conversation specifically because I'm afraid of being dismissed.' The specificity points to the actual need."]},
