            {"heading": "Recognizing Complicated Grief", "body": ["Complicated grief (also called prolonged grief disorder) is characterized by: intense grief that remains largely undiminished after 12 months (6 months in some criteria), significant impairment in daily functioning, persistent disbelief about the loss, avoidance of reminders or conversely, preoccupation with the deceased or lost object, and inability to experience positive emotions.", "Complicated grief affects approximately 10-15% of bereaved people. It is not a sign of weakness or excessive love — it is a specific clinical condition that responds well to targeted treatment (particularly Complicated Grief Treatment or CGT, a specific therapy protocol).", "If your grief is actively preventing you from functioning a year or more after loss, this is a signal to seek professional support — not as a sign that you're grieving incorrectly, but as compassionate care for yourself."]},
